WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

1
00:00:00.900 --> 00:00:02.343
Happiness isn't something, we're not a victim of it.

2
00:00:02.343 --> 00:00:04.300
It doesn't just happen to us.

3
00:00:04.300 --> 00:00:06.223
It's it's happening all the time.

4
00:00:06.223 --> 00:00:07.217
(upbeat music)

5
00:00:07.217 --> 00:00:09.570
The single most positive and effective tool

6
00:00:09.570 --> 00:00:11.530
is the five second rule.

7
00:00:11.530 --> 00:00:13.430
Whatever situation happened,

8
00:00:13.430 --> 00:00:15.530
didn't happen to you, it happened for you.

9
00:00:16.400 --> 00:00:19.370
And I could see that those who were over-managed

10
00:00:19.370 --> 00:00:21.183
were lacking an agency.

11
00:00:22.080 --> 00:00:24.513
Sleep is the single most effective thing

12
00:00:24.513 --> 00:00:26.450
that we can do each day

13
00:00:26.450 --> 00:00:29.957
to reset the health of our brain and our body.

14
00:00:32.060 --> 00:00:33.210
Happy holidays, everybody.

15
00:00:33.210 --> 00:00:34.120
Welcome.

16
00:00:34.120 --> 00:00:36.540
I must say it has been fun

17
00:00:36.540 --> 00:00:38.550
revisiting these conversations

18
00:00:38.550 --> 00:00:42.040
and splicing together this special episode.

19
00:00:42.040 --> 00:00:45.830
2021 was a transformative year in so many ways.

20
00:00:45.830 --> 00:00:49.540 line:15%
And part two of this recap tradition does not disappoint.

21
00:00:49.540 --> 00:00:52.810
Today, as I look back on the year,

22
00:00:52.810 --> 00:00:56.090
I'm just filled with so much gratitude,

23
00:00:56.090 --> 00:00:59.080
not just for all of the incredible individuals

24
00:00:59.080 --> 00:01:00.940
who have shared their time and their wisdom

25
00:01:00.940 --> 00:01:02.720
and their hearts with me,

26
00:01:02.720 --> 00:01:06.780
but really for all of you guys, for the listeners,

27
00:01:06.780 --> 00:01:10.070
I don't take your attention and support for granted.

28
00:01:10.070 --> 00:01:12.050
I relish in our community.

29
00:01:12.050 --> 00:01:14.340
And this recap practice

30
00:01:14.340 --> 00:01:18.900
is just one way that I can honor all of you,

31
00:01:18.900 --> 00:01:20.950
a way of saying thank you

32
00:01:20.950 --> 00:01:23.080
in this spirit of positive change,

33
00:01:23.080 --> 00:01:26.410
because I believe in the power we all have

34
00:01:26.410 --> 00:01:30.560
to do and be better and to own an actualize our best,

35
00:01:30.560 --> 00:01:32.300
most authentic selves.

36
00:01:32.300 --> 00:01:34.060
And so with that being said,

37
00:01:34.060 --> 00:01:36.380
what better way to kick things off today

38
00:01:36.380 --> 00:01:39.400
than by sharing the wisdom of author

39
00:01:39.400 --> 00:01:43.210
and one of my personal heroes, Steven Pressfield.

40
00:01:43.210 --> 00:01:46.190
Revered for his creative prolificacy,

41
00:01:46.190 --> 00:01:48.410
Stephen has 20 books to his name,

42
00:01:48.410 --> 00:01:52.130
including a book that radically changed my life.

43
00:01:52.130 --> 00:01:55.327
A book that I gift more than any other book,

44
00:01:55.327 --> 00:01:57.360
"The War of Art."

45
00:01:57.360 --> 00:02:00.180
Steven joined me on episode 584,

46
00:02:00.180 --> 00:02:02.180
serving up truth, big and small,

47
00:02:02.180 --> 00:02:04.360
on honing creativity,

48
00:02:04.360 --> 00:02:06.290
overcoming resistance,

49
00:02:06.290 --> 00:02:08.690
the importance of persistence

50
00:02:08.690 --> 00:02:12.893
and why art is about discipline, not talent.

51
00:02:14.120 --> 00:02:15.750 line:15%
Kindly hit that subscribe button,

52
00:02:15.750 --> 00:02:17.800 line:15%
and please enjoy this excerpt

53
00:02:17.800 --> 00:02:20.430 line:15%
from my conversation with Steven Pressfield.

54
00:02:21.750 --> 00:02:24.580
I call it resistance with a capital R

55
00:02:24.580 --> 00:02:27.010
and like if we had a typewriter

56
00:02:27.010 --> 00:02:28.670
or a keyboard in front of me now,

57
00:02:28.670 --> 00:02:30.150
or you've got one there,

58
00:02:30.150 --> 00:02:33.080
with a blank screen or a blank page in it,

59
00:02:33.080 --> 00:02:35.170
you would feel, we would feel,

60
00:02:35.170 --> 00:02:38.960
a force radiating off that page, a negative force,

61
00:02:38.960 --> 00:02:40.990
trying to push you away from it, right.

62
00:02:40.990 --> 00:02:44.000
And it would take, that's what I call resistance.

63
00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:46.180
It would be the same thing as if

64
00:02:46.180 --> 00:02:49.680
we went out and bought an exercise bike or a treadmill,

65
00:02:49.680 --> 00:02:51.780
and we brought it home to the house, oh, we're going to,

66
00:02:51.780 --> 00:02:53.560
and suddenly we realize we're coming up

67
00:02:53.560 --> 00:02:56.450
with every excuse in the world not to get on that treadmill.

68
00:02:56.450 --> 00:03:01.450
So resistance is this negative force of self-sabotage

69
00:03:02.180 --> 00:03:05.760
that will work against us

70
00:03:05.760 --> 00:03:09.560
anytime we try to move from a lower level to a higher level,

71
00:03:09.560 --> 00:03:11.760
ethically, morally, creatively.

72
00:03:11.760 --> 00:03:13.790
If you have an idea for a book,

73
00:03:13.790 --> 00:03:15.610
if you have an idea for a podcast,

74
00:03:15.610 --> 00:03:17.210
if you have an idea for this studio

75
00:03:17.210 --> 00:03:18.600
or something that you want to do,

76
00:03:18.600 --> 00:03:21.490
and I want to ask you about this Rich,

77
00:03:21.490 --> 00:03:25.640
a voice will come into your head immediately that will say,

78
00:03:25.640 --> 00:03:28.580
who are you to do, put this thing together.

79
00:03:28.580 --> 00:03:30.240
This has been done a million times

80
00:03:30.240 --> 00:03:32.600
and it's been done better than you ever could do

81
00:03:32.600 --> 00:03:33.433
or ever would do.

82
00:03:33.433 --> 00:03:34.350
You're too old.

83
00:03:34.350 --> 00:03:35.183
You're too young.

84
00:03:35.183 --> 00:03:36.016
You're too fat.

85
00:03:36.016 --> 00:03:36.849
You're too skinny.

86
00:03:36.849 --> 00:03:37.682
You don't have enough education.

87
00:03:37.682 --> 00:03:40.450
You have too much education, et cetera, et cetera.

88
00:03:40.450 --> 00:03:44.380
And that negative force is universal.

89
00:03:44.380 --> 00:03:47.330
I can tell you from the thousands of emails I've got,

90
00:03:47.330 --> 00:03:49.740
and not only is it universal,

91
00:03:49.740 --> 00:03:53.900
but it's the same voice in every, in all of our heads.

92
00:03:53.900 --> 00:03:56.880
You know, it may be tailored a little bit to you or to me,

93
00:03:56.880 --> 00:03:58.820
but it's, it's the same voice.

94
00:03:58.820 --> 00:04:01.440
And I was never aware of that.

95
00:04:01.440 --> 00:04:05.405
When I first started to write as a 24 year old,

96
00:04:05.405 --> 00:04:09.060
resistance just kicked my ass all over the place.

97
00:04:09.060 --> 00:04:11.330
And I, I, you know,

98
00:04:11.330 --> 00:04:12.520
I went through a lot of stuff

99
00:04:12.520 --> 00:04:15.180
before I finally kind of said to myself,

100
00:04:15.180 --> 00:04:16.690
you know what, there's a force out there

101
00:04:16.690 --> 00:04:18.400
that's working against me.

102
00:04:18.400 --> 00:04:19.510
You know, it's not just,

103
00:04:19.510 --> 00:04:22.220
it's not just something I'm inventing.

104
00:04:22.220 --> 00:04:25.210
There is a real force out there just like gravity,

105
00:04:25.210 --> 00:04:28.750
just like, you know, the transit of Venus across the sky.

106
00:04:28.750 --> 00:04:32.032
And once I could sort of give a name to it, then,

107
00:04:32.032 --> 00:04:33.930
then I could say, okay,

108
00:04:33.930 --> 00:04:35.910
now I have something I can deal with.

109
00:04:35.910 --> 00:04:37.630
How can I overcome this?

110
00:04:37.630 --> 00:04:40.950
Can I develop habits that will help me overcome it?

111
00:04:40.950 --> 00:04:44.150
Can I organize my day in such a way?

112
00:04:44.150 --> 00:04:47.210
Can I change my mindset in such a way?

113
00:04:47.210 --> 00:04:49.791
And so, anyway, that's kind of my definition of resistance.

114
00:04:49.791 --> 00:04:50.624
Right.

115
00:04:50.624 --> 00:04:52.971
Well, the first step seems to be

116
00:04:52.971 --> 00:04:57.971
disassociating your identity from the resistance itself,

117
00:04:58.340 --> 00:05:01.513
because I think what we all kind of do is,

118
00:05:03.477 --> 00:05:04.790
is self identify with that.

119
00:05:04.790 --> 00:05:06.095
That is part of the--
That's a great way

120
00:05:06.095 --> 00:05:06.928
of putting it, Richard.

121
00:05:06.928 --> 00:05:07.761
I've never heard that before.

122
00:05:07.761 --> 00:05:08.900
That's exactly it.

123
00:05:08.900 --> 00:05:10.680
Well, you have talked about, you know,

124
00:05:10.680 --> 00:05:12.811
this idea that exists outside of yourself, right.

125
00:05:12.811 --> 00:05:15.070
If you're just thinking, well, I can't do it.

126
00:05:15.070 --> 00:05:16.491
This is me telling myself this,

127
00:05:16.491 --> 00:05:19.880
as opposed to this external force

128
00:05:19.880 --> 00:05:21.130
that we can, you know,

129
00:05:21.130 --> 00:05:25.890
define as this pernicious entity working at odds with our,

130
00:05:25.890 --> 00:05:30.423
this, our effort to climb to that, you know, elevated place.

131
00:05:31.500 --> 00:05:35.670
But what was it that was like the light switch for you

132
00:05:35.670 --> 00:05:38.610
that allowed you to kind of come to that realization?

133
00:05:38.610 --> 00:05:40.410
Was it just pain?

134
00:05:40.410 --> 00:05:42.133
You know, it was pain, I guess, but, you know,

135
00:05:42.133 --> 00:05:43.280
I can't actually remember.

136
00:05:43.280 --> 00:05:44.990
There was like a moment.
Right.

137
00:05:44.990 --> 00:05:46.851
When I said that, or if there was to myself, oh,

138
00:05:46.851 --> 00:05:49.400
this is resistance.

139
00:05:49.400 --> 00:05:52.991
It just, you know, just over time, I guess.

140
00:05:52.991 --> 00:05:54.680
I mean, there was a moment

141
00:05:54.680 --> 00:05:56.960
that sort of where things turned around in that way for me,

142
00:05:56.960 --> 00:05:57.990
but I don't think I,

143
00:05:57.990 --> 00:06:00.720
I identified a force as resistance.

144
00:06:00.720 --> 00:06:02.860
But what you just said, Richard, is exactly right,

145
00:06:02.860 --> 00:06:06.817
the, of disassociating this concept of resistance,

146
00:06:06.817 --> 00:06:10.070
this fact of resistance from your own identity.

147
00:06:10.070 --> 00:06:12.700
Like when we hear this voice in our head

148
00:06:12.700 --> 00:06:14.780
that says you're not good enough,

149
00:06:14.780 --> 00:06:17.180
it's all been done, et cetera, et cetera.

150
00:06:17.180 --> 00:06:19.390
What makes that so powerful against us

151
00:06:19.390 --> 00:06:21.610
is we think it's our own thoughts.

152
00:06:21.610 --> 00:06:25.810
We think, oh, that's me assessing the situation objectively,

153
00:06:25.810 --> 00:06:26.930
but it's not.

154
00:06:26.930 --> 00:06:30.340
It's this other siren voice, this,

155
00:06:30.340 --> 00:06:32.090
this force that's just out there.

156
00:06:32.090 --> 00:06:33.347
That's a fact of nature.

157
00:06:33.347 --> 00:06:36.353
And once we can say, oh, that's not me.

158
00:06:37.730 --> 00:06:39.880
That kind of is the key to the whole thing.

159
00:06:40.900 --> 00:06:43.480
Next up is a really powerful clip

160
00:06:43.480 --> 00:06:44.950
that's excerpted from perhaps

161
00:06:44.950 --> 00:06:48.110
