162
00:06:48.110 --> 00:06:50.910
coming from elite marathoner and ultra marathoner

163
00:06:50.910 --> 00:06:53.010
Tommy Rivers Puzey

164
00:06:53.010 --> 00:06:55.350
offering poignant words of wisdom

165
00:06:55.350 --> 00:06:57.730
gleaned from his recent near-fatal battle

166
00:06:57.730 --> 00:07:00.543
with a rare and aggressive form of lymphoma.

167
00:07:02.710 --> 00:07:06.413
You know, I remember being very aware that it was serious.

168
00:07:06.413 --> 00:07:07.890
And any, anytime you have something

169
00:07:07.890 --> 00:07:10.970
that's going on with your respiratory system, you,

170
00:07:10.970 --> 00:07:12.820
you really quickly lose your ego.

171
00:07:12.820 --> 00:07:15.330
I mean, you, there's not much bartering.

172
00:07:15.330 --> 00:07:16.163
Then you can't,

173
00:07:16.163 --> 00:07:17.840
you can't really tough your way through that.

174
00:07:17.840 --> 00:07:20.590
You know, you take away somebody's oxygen and it's just

175
00:07:22.210 --> 00:07:23.850
a matter of minutes and you're,

176
00:07:23.850 --> 00:07:26.150
you're begging for somebody, something,

177
00:07:26.150 --> 00:07:27.239
anything to save you.

178
00:07:27.239 --> 00:07:28.563
I mean, that's why,

179
00:07:30.380 --> 00:07:32.060
it's like being waterboarded, you know.

180
00:07:32.060 --> 00:07:35.870
And, and there's a reason why, gosh,

181
00:07:35.870 --> 00:07:39.410
that's so effective because there's this,

182
00:07:39.410 --> 00:07:42.660
there's this fear that comes over you when

183
00:07:44.190 --> 00:07:47.340
everything protective within your body is telling you

184
00:07:47.340 --> 00:07:48.500
you're going to die.

185
00:07:48.500 --> 00:07:50.600
Like you're, you've got seconds, you know,

186
00:07:51.890 --> 00:07:55.053
unless you change something and,

187
00:07:55.970 --> 00:07:58.360
you know, you become desperate and,

188
00:07:58.360 --> 00:07:59.450
and whatever it is, you know,

189
00:07:59.450 --> 00:08:03.680
whatever it is that that needs to be done to,

190
00:08:03.680 --> 00:08:07.462
to be able to make you stop feeling that you're willing to,

191
00:08:07.462 --> 00:08:08.547
you're willing to do it.

192
00:08:08.547 --> 00:08:09.380
You're willing to concede.

193
00:08:09.380 --> 00:08:10.213
And

194
00:08:12.170 --> 00:08:13.412
yeah.

195
00:08:13.412 --> 00:08:14.762
And yeah, that was kind of,

196
00:08:15.720 --> 00:08:19.379
it's kind of where things started to just go blurry for me,

197
00:08:19.379 --> 00:08:20.212
'cause

198
00:08:22.320 --> 00:08:23.273
after that, it was,

199
00:08:25.740 --> 00:08:27.083
that was in July.

200
00:08:28.900 --> 00:08:30.680
I think it was probably

201
00:08:33.040 --> 00:08:34.903
November before I.

202
00:08:36.310 --> 00:08:38.820
Like your next memory.
Yeah, before I finally--

203
00:08:38.820 --> 00:08:40.830
Where you in and out of consciousness?

204
00:08:40.830 --> 00:08:41.720
Yeah.
You were,

205
00:08:41.720 --> 00:08:44.940
you just don't have recollection of that.

206
00:08:44.940 --> 00:08:45.790
I do.

207
00:08:45.790 --> 00:08:47.940
That's where things get really,

208
00:08:47.940 --> 00:08:48.920
really

209
00:08:50.410 --> 00:08:51.643
difficult to decipher.

210
00:08:55.735 --> 00:08:58.470
What it feels like is, I mean, I've been told now that it,

211
00:08:58.470 --> 00:09:01.500
that it's amnesia, retrograde amnesia and, you know,

212
00:09:01.500 --> 00:09:05.740
not having enough oxygen for so many weeks and months

213
00:09:05.740 --> 00:09:07.330
that there's, you know,

214
00:09:07.330 --> 00:09:10.460
irreparable brain damage and things like that.

215
00:09:10.460 --> 00:09:11.293
And

216
00:09:13.225 --> 00:09:16.940
what it felt like was that all of these files that I had

217
00:09:18.640 --> 00:09:20.110
over the last year,

218
00:09:20.110 --> 00:09:23.070
like if I can have a year and then,

219
00:09:23.070 --> 00:09:25.310
you know, I'll gladly go.

220
00:09:25.310 --> 00:09:27.560
And I've made it, it's been a year.

221
00:09:27.560 --> 00:09:28.393
Yeah.

222
00:09:30.270 --> 00:09:31.410
Gosh, and then you get selfish.

223
00:09:31.410 --> 00:09:32.690
You're like, okay, five more,

224
00:09:32.690 --> 00:09:33.827
20 more, 50 more.
Right.

225
00:09:33.827 --> 00:09:35.760
You know.
Cause we're humans, man.

226
00:09:35.760 --> 00:09:36.593
Right.

227
00:09:36.593 --> 00:09:37.509
Exactly.

228
00:09:37.509 --> 00:09:39.110
But I mean, it, it doesn't,

229
00:09:39.110 --> 00:09:40.200
it doesn't get any better than this.

230
00:09:40.200 --> 00:09:41.510
It's, it really doesn't.

231
00:09:41.510 --> 00:09:43.363
And I mean, the tragedy is that we,

232
00:09:47.362 --> 00:09:50.520
that we don't see it until afterwards, you know.

233
00:09:50.520 --> 00:09:52.120
I mean, I've been thinking about

234
00:09:55.340 --> 00:09:57.470
people say like, and they lived

235
00:09:58.310 --> 00:10:01.740
and they lived happily ever after like the,

236
00:10:01.740 --> 00:10:04.130
the operative verb there is lived, you know.

237
00:10:04.130 --> 00:10:05.520
They were,

238
00:10:05.520 --> 00:10:08.320
they were alive and that's why,

239
00:10:08.320 --> 00:10:09.993
that's why they were able to,

240
00:10:11.070 --> 00:10:13.270
that's why it was happily ever after.

241
00:10:13.270 --> 00:10:16.149
Then they died and it becomes a fucking tragedy, you know.

242
00:10:16.149 --> 00:10:17.130
Right.

243
00:10:17.130 --> 00:10:19.023
But like, gosh,

244
00:10:23.140 --> 00:10:24.230
life is incredible.

245
00:10:24.230 --> 00:10:25.593
I mean, it, it just,

246
00:10:27.720 --> 00:10:30.053
as bad as it seems, as hard as it seems,

247
00:10:32.720 --> 00:10:34.003
it's just such a,

248
00:10:35.810 --> 00:10:37.440
a gift to be able to do it.

249
00:10:37.440 --> 00:10:38.880
And we talk about

250
00:10:43.530 --> 00:10:45.383
how tragic it is when somebody,

251
00:10:46.980 --> 00:10:48.470
when something happens, you know, somebody dies,

252
00:10:48.470 --> 00:10:49.720
somebody gets sick,

253
00:10:49.720 --> 00:10:51.720
there's an accident, there's a disaster.

254
00:10:52.747 --> 00:10:53.580
And as though,

255
00:10:58.448 --> 00:10:59.865
as though that's,

256
00:11:03.760 --> 00:11:05.383
that's out of the norm and,

257
00:11:06.528 --> 00:11:07.720
and in reality,

258
00:11:07.720 --> 00:11:09.580
the fact that we're alive, it's just,

259
00:11:09.580 --> 00:11:10.870
it's just a miracle.

260
00:11:10.870 --> 00:11:12.880
I mean, the fact that we're,

261
00:11:12.880 --> 00:11:14.280
that we're here, that we're,

262
00:11:16.910 --> 00:11:18.240
becomes hard to talk about this stuff

263
00:11:18.240 --> 00:11:20.200
because you can't express it in ways

